1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,420
Welcome to DeadBolt, a free, open
source execution gate from NextEleven.

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:11,000
This walkthrough starts with the problem,
shows the integration choices, and then

3
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:15,940
demonstrates real allowed and blocked
effects. DeadBolt gives an operator control

4
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:20,720
over a particular agent's next protected
action. It works with a trusted

5
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:26,460
dispatcher, whether your application uses
Rust, a local sidecar, an MCP server,

6
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:31,960
or supported Python function tools. We'll
finish inside the actual Harness executable

7
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:36,840
and its web interface. The model
responses in those examples come from

8
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:42,600
a deterministic local fixture. The files,
operator commands, admission decisions,

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,500
and tool execution are real. Cedar
provides the AI generated narration. The

10
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:53,080
visual presentation is an educational
demo, separate from the shipping product

11
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:53,520
interface.

12
00:00:53,882 --> 00:00:59,422
Exact action approval binds the
executor's run, routed tool, destination,

13
00:01:00,182 --> 00:01:05,602
validated JSON arguments, a unique
nonce, and a fixed deadline. Prepare the

14
00:01:05,602 --> 00:01:10,602
review request in trusted code, and
protect the private review file. Inspect

15
00:01:10,602 --> 00:01:15,302
the actual content and its fingerprint.
The operator approves that reviewed

16
00:01:15,302 --> 00:01:20,222
fingerprint. The dispatch helper later
uses a copied argument snapshot for the

17
00:01:20,222 --> 00:01:25,082
body. It must not read different
recipients or amounts from mutable state.

18
00:01:26,102 --> 00:01:30,482
Changing reviewed content does not
match the grant and is refused. The

19
00:01:30,482 --> 00:01:36,182
original reviewed write succeeds once; its
replay is refused. This demonstration uses

20
00:01:36,182 --> 00:01:41,342
a temporary file transport, not email.
Rebuild a request with every effect

21
00:01:41,342 --> 00:01:46,362
affecting value resolved, and version
your tool identity when its schema or

22
00:01:46,362 --> 00:01:46,882
behavior changes.

23
00:01:47,148 --> 00:01:52,648
Now we're using the optimized Harness
executable copied outside its source checkout.

24
00:01:53,528 --> 00:01:58,628
Its explicit demo configuration enables
DeadBolt and fail closed, with isolated

25
00:01:58,628 --> 00:02:03,568
state. A synthetic loopback provider
proposes a normal file write.

26
00:02:04,748 --> 00:02:08,948
Harness runs the protected tool, and
the file appears. Before the next

27
00:02:08,948 --> 00:02:13,868
proposed write, the operator kills that
run. Harness reports a killed denial,

28
00:02:14,148 --> 00:02:19,148
and the second file does not exist.
A different Harness agent then

29
00:02:19,148 --> 00:02:23,448
writes its own file successfully.
The output on screen is captured from

30
00:02:23,448 --> 00:02:29,388
actual commands and tool results, with
demo paths shortened for readability. This

31
00:02:29,388 --> 00:02:33,488
is a deterministic integration test,
not a claim about a particular model's

32
00:02:33,488 --> 00:02:38,948
behavior. Harness currently uses legacy
admission and correctly refuses exact only

33
00:02:38,948 --> 00:02:42,168
tools until a dedicated exact
action adapter is added.

34
00:02:42,362 --> 00:02:47,422
Developers can also work through Harness's
actual web interface. This screenshot

35
00:02:47,422 --> 00:02:52,242
comes from the running application, not
a proposed DeadBolt dashboard. We asked

36
00:02:52,242 --> 00:02:55,782
the local fixture to read a
harmless document and then attempt a

37
00:02:55,782 --> 00:02:59,742
write. The read went through the
permitted tool. The write was refused

38
00:02:59,742 --> 00:03:04,302
with purpose exceeded, and the output
file was independently checked to be

39
00:03:04,302 --> 00:03:09,482
absent. Sessions, the conversation,
and activity events remain visible.

40
00:03:09,482 --> 00:03:14,062
A done event means the tool
call returned; it does not by

41
00:03:14,062 --> 00:03:18,562
itself mean the requested effect succeeded.
The application should expose the

42
00:03:18,562 --> 00:03:24,222
decision clearly. DeadBolt's operator
control is still separate from the chat. This

43
00:03:24,222 --> 00:03:28,782
demo interface uses the same protected
execution boundary, with a deterministic

44
00:03:28,782 --> 00:03:30,902
provider and dedicated demo data.

45
00:03:31,123 --> 00:03:35,903
Start with one narrow tool route in
your application. Choose the released

46
00:03:35,903 --> 00:03:41,143
or reviewed candidate API, configure a
private store, assign a stable identity,

47
00:03:41,623 --> 00:03:46,863
and set a useful policy. Wrap the
actual effect function and test

48
00:03:46,863 --> 00:03:51,903
its allowed behavior. Then verify
policy denial, operator revocation,

49
00:03:52,883 --> 00:03:58,343
expiry, approval replay, and gate outage
without accepting a hidden fallback. Add

50
00:03:58,343 --> 00:04:03,383
exact content review when arguments
determine a sensitive outcome. Add scoped

51
00:04:03,383 --> 00:04:08,783
credentials when workloads should lack
operator authority. Record which routes are

52
00:04:08,783 --> 00:04:13,483
covered, and expand only after their
effects are checked. DeadBolt is free

53
00:04:13,483 --> 00:04:17,963
under Apache two point zero, with no
model subscription required for the

54
00:04:17,963 --> 00:04:23,723
gate. The guide, reproducible fixtures,
source revisions, captions, and

55
00:04:23,723 --> 00:04:29,383
evidence accompany this walkthrough. Your
next step is a small independently verified

56
00:04:29,383 --> 00:04:29,843
integration.
